\subsection{Symbolic Confirmation Bias}
\label{sec:confirmation-bias}

\looseness=-1 Self-extracted constraints systematically confirm the model's majority answer. On Mistral-7B FOLIO, when SICA errs, 83.3\% of constraints support the incorrect answer---bias ratio $4.97\times$ (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias})---and 94\% of errors overlap with SC. Z3 rejects only 3.4\% of constraints, confirming degeneration (Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}).

\looseness=-1 Bias generalizes: $4.97\times$ [3.98, 6.37] (Mistral, 12-trace), $3.93\times$ [2.44, 7.11] (Qwen-14B, 12-trace), $2.88\times$ (LLaMA, 4-trace$^{\dag}$), all $> 1.0$ (95\% bootstrap CIs for Mistral/Qwen; Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias}; weighted BRs in Appendix~\ref{app:cross-model-bias}). Constraints use trace-specific variables (56.4\% zero cross-answer overlap), collapsing aggregation to majority voting.

\looseness=-1 Process-conditional decomposition reveals that SCB is not a local artifact of answer leakage: BR is uniform across early ($5.37$), mid ($5.25$), and late ($5.16$) reasoning steps (Fisher $p = 0.71$), confirming that bias is present from the first extraction step. Conditioning on difficulty amplifies the pattern: high-consensus errors (SC $\geq 10/12$) exhibit $\text{BR} = 18.8\times$, versus $2.7\times$ for low-consensus errors (SC $\leq 6/12$; $p < 0.001$)---indicating near-complete capture of FOL extraction by incorrect reasoning when the model is most confident.

\looseness=-1 Answer-free extraction corroborates this finding. We truncate each CoT trace by removing the final answer sentence before constraint extraction (three strategies: conservative, standard, aggressive), testing whether SCB arises from explicit answer leakage. Across all strategies, BR shows no significant decrease (Wilcoxon one-sided $p > 0.12$; full-trace $\text{BR} = 2.79$, truncated $\text{BR} = 2.26$--$2.66$), while accuracy drops significantly (McNemar $p < 0.013$; $59.31\% \to 53.92$--$54.41\%$). SCB thus reflects a distributional prior embedded throughout reasoning, not answer-sentence contamination.

\subsection{Process-Level Independence}
\label{sec:process-kappa}

We define process similarity as the mean pairwise cosine similarity of TF-IDF trace representations:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:process-sim}
\text{ProcessSim}(q) = \frac{2}{K(K{-}1)} \sum_{i < j} \cos(\mathbf{v}_i^q, \mathbf{v}_j^q),
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{v}_i^q$ is the TF-IDF vector of trace $t_i$ for question $q$. On Mistral-7B FOLIO, mean ProcessSim $= 0.69$, far exceeding answer-level $\kappa = 0.46$: traces share reasoning processes even when answers diverge. The gap generalizes across four families (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-process}), ranging from $0.31$ (LLaMA) to $0.02$ (Qwen-14B), revealing hidden process homogeneity that answer diversity masks.


\subsection{Supporting Diagnostics}
\label{sec:diagnostics}

Further diagnostics confirm the mechanism (Appendices~\ref{app:extraction}--\ref{app:entropy}). One positive signal emerges: Z3-based semantic deduplication removes ${\sim}$10\% additional constraints beyond syntactic dedup and yields a small but significant gain ($p = 0.013$), confirming that the formal channel adds value when redundancy is reduced. The bottleneck is not Z3 but the quality of input constraints: confirmation bias ($\text{BR} = 4.97\times$) overwhelms the deduplication gain.


\subsection{Remediation Analysis}
\label{sec:remediation}
\label{sec:interventions}

\looseness=-1 Twenty-seven remediation strategies spanning four categories---sampling diversity (temperature, multi-T, diverse prompting), extraction quality (few-shot, iterative refinement, confidence filtering), aggregation (constraint weighting, semantic dedup, MAX-SAT variants), and multi-agent (debate, self-refine, critique)---all produce neutral-to-negative results (Figure~\ref{fig:interventions-summary}; Tables~\ref{tab:method_interventions}, \ref{tab:remediation}, \ref{tab:additional_interventions}). Of these, 12 represent principled strategies targeting distinct failure modes (independent constraint generation, contrastive extraction, debate-augmented reasoning, etc.), while 15 are parameter variations (temperature, prompt template, few-shot count). At the method level, Debate achieves the lowest $\kappa = 0.15$ but collapses accuracy by $-18.6$\,pp ($p < 0.001$); all other methods cluster at $\kappa \approx 0.40$--$0.47$, confirming a same-model ceiling (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:kappa_generalization}). We note an important gap: we do not evaluate trained constraint extractors (e.g., fine-tuned on oracle annotations). Our oracle controls show the extraction-quality ceiling is $+29.90$\,pp---we consider trained extractors the most promising future direction.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{figures/fig_app_interventions_summary.pdf}
\caption{Summary of 27 intervention strategies grouped by four categories. All produce neutral-to-negative accuracy changes relative to Self-Consistency. The grey band indicates $\pm 1$\,pp noise zone.}
\label{fig:interventions-summary}
\end{figure*}

\section{Cross-Architecture Proof-of-Concept}
\label{sec:solutions}
\label{sec:cross-arch}

\paragraph{Method.}
We test with DeBERTa-large \citep{he2021deberta} (406M, encoder-only), fine-tuned on MultiNLI \citep{williams2018mnli}, classifying (premise, conclusion) pairs as entailment, contradiction, or neutral. In combo mode, each NLI-supporting vote counts $w$ times alongside SC's $K$ votes. We evaluate on Mistral-7B FOLIO ($n = 204$) and ProofWriter depth-5 ($n = 600$), whose (premise, conclusion) structure suits NLI classification.

\paragraph{Main result.}
\looseness=-1 DeBERTa NLI alone achieves 49.51\% on FOLIO (below SC's 55.88\%) and 55.00\% on ProofWriter (above SC's 39.33\%). On FOLIO ($n = 204$), combo mode yields $+0.49$\,pp at $w{=}1$ and $+2.94$\,pp at $w{=}3$ (both n.s.). On ProofWriter ($n = 600$), $w{=}3$ achieves $+6.67$\,pp ($p < 0.001$) and $w{=}5$ reaches $+11.00$\,pp ($p < 0.001$)---while same-model SICA \emph{degrades} ProofWriter ($-2.83$\,pp, raw $p = 0.014$; BH adj.\ $p = 0.112$; Table~\ref{tab:main_results}), illustrating that corrective signal must originate outside the model's distribution (\S\ref{sec:mirage}). We report $w{=}3$ as primary. After BH correction ($m{=}12$), Mistral-7B $w{=}3$ (adj.\ $p = 6.78 \times 10^{-5}$) and $w{=}5$ (adj.\ $p = 7.41 \times 10^{-6}$) remain significant; all other conditions are non-significant.

\paragraph{Why it works.}
Three conditions jointly enable the escape: (i)~\emph{structural independence}---encoder-only DeBERTa and decoder-only Mistral share no architecture or training data (\S\ref{sec:mirage}); (ii)~\emph{positive capability gap}---verifier standalone accuracy exceeds generator SC (Finding~2); (iii)~\emph{task-verifier alignment}---NLI classification naturally matches premise-conclusion structure. Per-label complementarity: NLI excels on \textsc{False} (71.43\% vs.\ 38.10\%), SC dominates \textsc{Unknown} (68.12\% vs.\ 18.84\%). All three NLI architectures produce significant gains at $w \geq 3$ (Appendix~\ref{app:multi-nli}).

\enlargethispage{\baselineskip}
\paragraph{Capability gap hypothesis.}
\looseness=-1 Four generators spanning a 46-point SC range on ProofWriter ($n{=}600$), all paired with DeBERTa-large-mnli (55.00\% standalone; Table~\ref{tab:cross_generator_nli}): only Mistral-7B (SC 39.33\%, below the verifier) gains significantly ($+6.67$\,pp, $p < 0.001$); LLaMA-3.1-8B ($+0.83$), Qwen2.5-14B ($+0.67$), and Qwen3-14B ($+0.50$\,pp) show no improvement. After BH correction ($m{=}12$), only Mistral-7B survives. The monotonic relationship holds across three NLI architectures, spanning six verifier-generator pairs (Spearman $\rho = 1.0$, $p = 0.003$, $n{=}6$; four of six share the DeBERTa verifier---see Limitations; Figure~\ref{fig:capability-gap}, Appendix~\ref{app:capability-gap}), confirming Finding~2.

